          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In locked-in syndrome caused by a pontine haemorrhage, which voluntary movement is characteristically preserved?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Horizontal eye movements</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF), the horizontal gaze centre, lies within the pons and is destroyed by the haemorrhage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Swallowing</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Corticobulbar fibres passing through the basis pontis are destroyed bilaterally, impairing voluntary control of speech and swallowing.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vertical eye movements and blinking</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Vertical gaze is controlled by the midbrain, which lies above the pontine lesion and is therefore spared.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Finger flexion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bilateral destruction of the corticospinal tracts in the basis pontis produces complete quadriplegia, abolishing all limb movements.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is vertical eye movements and blinking. The midbrain controls vertical gaze and lies above the pontine lesion, so these movements survive even when the pons is devastated."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Vertical eye movements and blinking survive because their control centres lie in the midbrain, one level above the destroyed pons — making them the patient's only window to the outside world."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
