          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with ipsilateral lateral rectus palsy and contralateral hemiplegia, but no facial weakness. Which pontine syndrome does this describe?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Foville syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Foville syndrome is more extensive — it involves the CN VI nucleus (causing horizontal gaze palsy), CN VII fascicles, the corticospinal tract, and the medial lemniscus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Locked-in syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Locked-in syndrome involves bilateral destruction of the basis pontis, resulting in quadriplegia and anarthria with preserved consciousness — a far more extensive lesion than described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Millard-Gubler syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Millard-Gubler syndrome includes ipsilateral facial palsy in addition to lateral rectus palsy and contralateral hemiplegia. The absence of facial weakness rules it out.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Raymond syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Raymond syndrome results from damage limited to the CN VI fascicles and the corticospinal tract, without involvement of cranial nerve VII — hence no facial weakness.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is Raymond syndrome. It involves only the CN VI fascicles and the corticospinal tract, sparing CN VII. The presence or absence of ipsilateral facial palsy is what separates Raymond from Millard-Gubler syndrome."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Raymond syndrome affects only the CN VI fascicles and the corticospinal tract. Adding ipsilateral facial palsy would make it Millard-Gubler syndrome — CN VII involvement is the key differentiator."
                }
              ],
